WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:16.890
<v Ira Glass>You may remember this story that we did about a year ago, about canvassers going door to door and talking to voters for 20 minutes. And in just 20 minutes, the canvassers got some voters to change their minds, to flip positions on big, hot button issues. Gay marriage. Abortion.
<v Ira Glass>你可能还记得我们大约在一年前做的那个报道，关于拉票员挨家挨户走访并与选民交谈20分钟的事。仅仅20分钟，拉票员就让一些选民改变了想法，在同性婚姻、堕胎等重大热点议题上转变了立场。

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:37.060
<v Ira Glass>Which, of course, is amazing, right? That is not something that seems like it's even possible. There was a study in a well-known, peer reviewed journal-- Science magazine-- proving how remarkably effective these people were. They invented a whole new way to sway voter opinion. It seemed like a game changer, a whole new tool for politics, for liberals or conservatives to use.
<v Ira Glass>这当然很神奇，对吧？这看起来甚至是不可能的事。在一份著名的同行评审期刊——《科学》杂志——上有一项研究，证明了这些人是多么显著地有效。他们发明了一种全新的方法来左右选民的观点。这看起来像是一个改变游戏规则的东西，是自由派或保守派可以使用的全新政治工具。

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:52.050
<v Ira Glass>And then a month after our radio story-- maybe you saw this in the news-- there was overwhelming evidence that one of the researchers, a grad student named Michael LaCour had fabricated all the data. Science magazine retracted the article.
<v Ira Glass>就在我们的广播报道播出一个月后——也许你在新闻里看到了——有压倒性的证据表明，其中一位研究人员，一名叫迈克尔·拉库尔的研究生，伪造了所有数据。《科学》杂志撤回了这篇文章。

00:00:52.050 --> 00:01:00.820
<v Ira Glass>But the team that invented this canvassing technique, they were not the ones that did the research study. That was a separate thing. And they were just as shocked as anybody. Maybe more shocked.
<v Ira Glass>但是，发明这种拉票技术的团队并不是做这项研究的人。那是另一码事。他们和任何人一样感到震惊。也许更震惊。

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:12.620
<v Ira Glass>The week the news broke, one of the canvass organizers, Steve DeLine, told me they'd spent months meticulously going door to door and keeping perfect records of everything they were doing for this researcher. To find out that LaCour had just made up his part of the data--
<v Ira Glass>新闻曝光的那一周，拉票组织者之一史蒂夫·德莱恩告诉我，他们花了几个月时间一丝不苟地挨家挨户走访，并为这位研究人员记录了他们所做的一切。得知拉库尔只是编造了他那部分数据——

00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:33.260
<v Steve Deline>It's unfathomable to me, having you know, made sure that we were dotting every I and crossing every T. And you know, jumping through every hoop that he asked of us. It's just-- just can't really explain it. I'm at a loss.
<v Steve Deline>这让我无法理解，你知道的，我们确保了所有细节都一丝不苟，完成了他要求的每一个环节。这真的……真的没法解释。我感到不知所措。

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:38.600
<v Ira Glass>Then in August, three months after the retraction, four months after our story aired--
<v Ira Glass>然后在八月，撤稿三个月后，我们的报道播出四个月后——

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.740
<v Ira Glass>Hi.
<v Ira Glass>嗨。

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:40.570
<v Dave Fleischer>Hi.
<v Dave Fleischer>嗨。

00:01:40.570 --> 00:01:40.990
<v Steve Deline>Steve.
<v Steve Deline>史蒂夫。

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:41.470
<v Dave Fleischer>Dave.
<v Dave Fleischer>戴夫。

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:42.270
<v Ira Glass>Hi.
<v Ira Glass>嗨。

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:42.860
<v Steve Deline>Nice to meet you.
<v Steve Deline>很高兴见到你。

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:43.870
<v Ira Glass>Nice to meet you guys in person.
<v Ira Glass>很高兴亲自见到你们。

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:54.430
<v Ira Glass>We got a visitor to our office from two of the guys who'd developed this canvassing technique, that guy Steve and his coworker Dave Fleischer. They happened to be in town from California. And they had news.
<v Ira Glass>我们办公室来了一位访客，是开发这种拉票技术的两个人之一，那个叫史蒂夫的家伙和他的同事戴夫·弗莱彻。他们正好从加州来城里。他们带来了消息。

00:01:54.430 --> 00:01:59.180
<v Ira Glass>A brand new study with new researchers was looking into their work. Results were coming in. Here's Dave.
<v Ira Glass>一项由新研究人员进行的全新研究正在审视他们的工作。结果正在汇总。这是戴夫。

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:07.590
<v Dave Fleischer>We're not anticipating going public with these results until January at the earliest, and maybe not till June.
<v Dave Fleischer>我们预计至少要到一月才会公布这些结果，也许要到六月。

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:17.380
<v Ira Glass>What these researchers really wanted to do was publish their results in Science magazine, you know, the place that published the original, discredited study. In fact, one of the researchers was laying the groundwork for that.
<v Ira Glass>这些研究人员真正想做的是在《科学》杂志上发表他们的结果，你知道的，那个刊登了最初那项被抹黑研究的地方。事实上，其中一位研究人员正在为此打基础。

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:36.680
<v Dave Fleischer>So he actually called up Marcia McNutt, who's the editor of Science. And they didn't immediately reject it out of hand. She just said, "You know, the bar's going to be very high." [LAUGHS] Well, no [BEEP] fooling.
<v Dave Fleischer>所以他真的给《科学》杂志的编辑玛西娅·麦克纳特打了电话。他们并没有立即断然拒绝。她只是说：“你知道，门槛会非常高。”（笑）好吧，毫无疑问。

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:43.990
<v Ira Glass>Well, apparently Science magazine liked what the new researchers did. This week they published the new study about these canvassers.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，显然《科学》杂志喜欢这些新研究人员所做的工作。本周他们发表了关于这些拉票员的新研究。

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:58.690
<v Ira Glass>This one's not fake. We know now, for real, whether those canvassers are, in fact, convincing anyone of anything when they go door to door. And if anything, what the canvassers are achieving and the way that they're achieving it is even more interesting than the fake study said.
<v Ira Glass>这篇不是假的。我们现在确实知道，这些拉票员挨家挨户走访时是否真的说服了任何人。而且如果说有什么的话，拉票员所取得的成就以及他们取得成就的方式，甚至比那项假研究说的还要有趣。

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:17.810
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago and This American Life, I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, "For Your Reconsideration." We have this story and two other stories where people go back and revisit evidence from the past. And just to start this off, in case you didn't hear our first story about those canvassers a year ago, here's what they were. If you heard that show, this will just be a quick reminder.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台和《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天我们的节目主题是“重新考虑”。我们有这个故事以及另外两个人们回顾过去证据的故事。首先，以防你没有听我们一年前关于那些拉票员的第一篇报道，这里是背景介绍。如果你听过那期节目，这只是一个简短的提醒。

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:23.080
<v Canvasser>Hi. We're talking to registered voters in your neighborhood today about their views on abortion.
<v Canvasser>嗨。我们今天正在与你们社区的登记选民谈论他们对堕胎的看法。

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:35.480
<v Ira Glass>This is one of the canvassers. She's young, with tattoos and a halter top. It's a warm Los Angeles day, and the voter's on the other side of a screen door. She's older, heavyset, wire rimmed glasses. She's a nurse.
<v Ira Glass>这是一位拉票员。她很年轻，有纹身，穿着挂脖上衣。这是洛杉矶温暖的一天，选民站在纱门后面。她年纪较大，身材魁梧，戴着细框眼镜。她是一名护士。

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:52.380
<v Ira Glass>And OK, just to be clear, what you're about to hear is a real canvasser with a real voter. This is not part of the discredited study. This is from a video of one of the regular canvasses these guys Dave and Steve organized. The canvasser begins by asking this voter what she believes about abortion on a scale from zero to 10.
<v Ira Glass>好的，为了明确起见，你接下来要听到的是一位真正的拉票员与一位真正的选民的对话。这不是那项欺诈性研究的一部分。这是来自戴夫和史蒂夫组织的常规拉票活动视频。拉票员首先询问这位选民在0到10的范围内对堕胎的看法。

00:03:52.380 --> 00:04:00.010
<v Canvasser>Zero means you think women should have no access to abortion. 10 means women should have full access to abortion.
<v Canvasser>0意味着你认为女性不应有堕胎权。10意味着女性应拥有完全的堕胎权。

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:06.280
<v Ira Glass>The voter says zero. No access. No abortions for anybody. She's Catholic, from Mexico.
<v Ira Glass>选民说0。没有堕胎权。任何人都不能堕胎。她是天主教徒，来自墨西哥。

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:10.060
<v Ira Glass>And they start talking about how hard it can be to discuss abortion. Even with family.
<v Ira Glass>他们开始谈论讨论堕胎有多困难。即使是和家人。

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:12.420
<v Canvasser>How did that feel for you to talk to your daughters?
<v Canvasser>和你的女儿们谈论这件事对你来说感觉如何？

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:28.350
<v Female Voter>It's not easy. It's not easy, because in my country they have taboos, a lot of taboos. And my mother is one of the person never talking about sexual relations or conceptions.
<v Female Voter>不容易。不容易，因为在我的国家有很多禁忌。我母亲是一个从不谈论性关系或受孕话题的人。

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:29.300
<v Canvasser>Yeah.
<v Canvasser>是的。

00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:32.450
<v Female Voter>That's why I try to be very open with my daughters.
<v Female Voter>这就是为什么我尽量对我的女儿们保持开放态度。

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:50.350
<v Ira Glass>Notice that this conversation's just begun and already she's talking about her daughters and her mom. That's no accident. This whole technique is built around having a very personal conversation, a very real conversation about the voter's experiences. To make that happen, this canvasser does something that a lot of the canvassers do-- she talks about her own experiences.
<v Ira Glass>注意到谈话才刚开始，她就已经在谈论她的女儿和母亲了。这不是偶然。整个技术建立在进行非常个人化、非常真实的对话，讨论选民经历的基础上。为了实现这一点，这位拉票员做了许多拉票员都会做的事——谈论她自己的经历。

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.940
<v Canvasser>My mother's from the Philippines.
<v Canvasser>我母亲来自菲律宾。

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:53.270
<v Female Voter>Uh-huh.
<v Female Voter>嗯。

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:59.130
<v Canvasser>And my mother went through the same thing. Like, it's not something that you talk about.
<v Canvasser>我母亲也经历了同样的事情。就像，这不是你可以谈论的事情。

00:04:59.130 --> 00:05:00.300
<v Female Voter>No. It's not.
<v Female Voter>不。确实不是。

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:00.600
<v Canvasser>Huh.
<v Canvasser>哈。

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:01.690
<v Female Voter>In my family, it's the same.
<v Female Voter>在我家里，情况也一样。

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:04.900
<v Canvasser>When I got my period, I was 12 years old.
<v Canvasser>我来月经的时候，我才12岁。

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:05.570
<v Female Voter>Mhm.
<v Female Voter>嗯。

00:05:05.570 --> 00:05:06.320
<v Canvasser>And--
<v Canvasser>而且——

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:12.060
<v Ira Glass>The voter tells her that when she was 11 and got her period, nobody explained what was happening. She thought she was dying.
<v Ira Glass>选民告诉她，她11岁来月经时，没人解释发生了什么。她以为自己要死了。

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.090
<v Female Voter>And I believe I am dying. [LAUGHS]
<v Female Voter>我以为我要死了。（笑）

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:16.060
<v Canvasser>Oh! [LAUGHS]
<v Canvasser>噢！（笑）

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:27.850
<v Ira Glass>And when she was six, her mom miscarried a baby. But nobody explained what was going on to her. And so she's tried to be different with her daughters and talk to them. That's why she became a nurse, she says, to help women.
<v Ira Glass>当她六岁时，她妈妈流产了。但没人向她解释发生了什么。所以她尽量以不同的方式对待她的女儿并与她们交谈。她说这就是她成为护士的原因，为了帮助女性。

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:38.600
<v Ira Glass>So these two strangers are swapping stories and really talking from the heart. And 15 minutes into this 22-minute conversation, the canvasser circles back to the subject at hand in a very pointed way.
<v Ira Glass>所以这两个陌生人在交换故事，真心实意地交流。在这场22分钟对话的第15分钟，拉票员以一种非常直接的方式回到了手头的话题。

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:40.270
<v Canvasser>I had an abortion.
<v Canvasser>我做过堕胎手术。

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:40.680
<v Female Voter>Mhm.
<v Female Voter>嗯。

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:42.500
<v Canvasser>In November this past year.
<v Canvasser>就在去年十一月。

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:43.590
<v Female Voter>Oh, I'm so sorry.
<v Female Voter>噢，我很抱歉。

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:46.320
<v Canvasser>And it wasn't the wrong choice for me.
<v Canvasser>对我来说，这不是错误的选择。

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:46.990
<v Female Voter>Mhm.
<v Female Voter>嗯。

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:50.890
<v Canvasser>Because that's what felt right for me. But I was alone.
<v Canvasser>因为那是我觉得正确的做法。但我一个人。

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:51.800
<v Female Voter>Yeah.
<v Female Voter>是的。

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:52.550
<v Canvasser>And it was scary.
<v Canvasser>而且很可怕。

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:52.860
<v Female Voter>Uh-huh.
<v Female Voter>嗯。

00:05:52.860 --> 00:06:01.540
<v Canvasser>And it wasn't-- it was because I don't know how to talk about it with people. Like, my family, my mother loves me. And so does my papa and my sister.
<v Canvasser>因为我不知道如何与别人谈论它。比如，我的家人，我妈妈爱我。我爸爸和妹妹也一样。

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:02.130
<v Female Voter>Mhm.
<v Female Voter>嗯。

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:02.520
<v Canvasser>But--
<v Canvasser>但是——

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:07.860
<v Female Voter>I know, but it's hard. And you carry it for the rest of your life. It's your decision--
<v Female Voter>我知道，但这很难。你得带着它过一辈子。这是你的决定——

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:08.520
<v Canvasser>Yeah.
<v Canvasser>是的。

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:12.010
<v Female Voter>But you carry it for the rest of your life.
<v Female Voter>但你得带着它过一辈子。

00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:19.350
<v Canvasser>If you are-- one of the things that I struggled with with telling my family is this idea that my family's going to love me less.
<v Canvasser>如果你——我告诉家人时挣扎的事情之一就是这种想法，即我的家人会不再爱我。

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:20.800
<v Female Voter>No. Never.
<v Female Voter>不。绝不会。

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.250
<v Canvasser>Would you ever love your daughters less?
<v Canvasser>你会少爱你的女儿们吗？

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:24.300
<v Female Voter>No. Never.
<v Female Voter>不。绝不会。

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:32.510
<v Ira Glass>It's a moment that's simultaneously intimate and manipulative and honest all at once. And it works.
<v Ira Glass>这是一个同时具有亲密性、操纵性和诚实性的时刻。而且它奏效了。

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:40.070
<v Ira Glass>After this, the canvasser asks the voter again to rate on a scale of zero to 10 where she stands on access to abortion. Remember, she was a zero before.
<v Ira Glass>之后，拉票员再次要求选民在0到10的范围内评估她对堕胎权的立场。记住，她之前是0。

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:45.970
<v Canvasser>We have that same zero to 10 scale where zero means no access and 10 means full access.
<v Canvasser>我们有同样的0到10量表，其中0意味着没有堕胎权，10意味着完全的堕胎权。

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:46.040
<v Female Voter>10.
<v Female Voter>10。

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:46.810
<v Canvasser>A 10?
<v Canvasser>10？

00:06:46.810 --> 00:06:47.890
<v Female Voter>Mhm.
<v Female Voter>嗯。

00:06:47.890 --> 00:06:54.510
<v Ira Glass>She's been standing at the door just 18 minutes. So again, OK, that's real. That is not part of the fraudulent study.
<v Ira Glass>她在门口才站了18分钟。所以，好吧，那是真实的。那不是欺诈性研究的一部分。

00:06:54.510 --> 00:07:19.950
<v Ira Glass>The team that invented this technique of going door to door-- including Dave Fleischer and Steve Deline, the two guys who visited our office at the beginning of this story-- they work at the Los Angeles LGBT Center, which is a big, multimillion-dollar nonprofit, the biggest LGBT organization in the world. They started doing these canvasses in 2009. They were hoping to change voters' minds to support gay marriage. This was in the wake of Proposition 8, which rejected gay marriage in California.
<v Ira Glass>发明这种挨家挨户拉票技术的团队——包括戴夫·弗莱彻和史蒂夫·德莱恩，也就是故事开头访问我们办公室的那两个人——他们在洛杉矶LGBT中心工作，这是一个大型的、价值数百万美元的非营利组织，是世界上最大的LGBT组织。他们从2009年开始进行这些拉票活动。他们希望改变选民的想法，以支持同性婚姻。这是在加州第8号提案（拒绝同性婚姻）之后发生的。

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:35.620
<v Ira Glass>And they discovered through trial and error that the trick to this whole thing was they don't try to reason with people about rights and equality and big principles like that. Canvass organizer Steve Deline says if you're talking at a kind of rational, sort of intellectual level about things, you're gonna fail.
<v Ira Glass>他们通过试错发现，整个事情的秘诀在于，他们不尝试在权利、平等和类似的大原则上与人们讲理。拉票组织者史蒂夫·德莱恩说，如果你在谈论理性、知识层面的东西，你注定会失败。

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:46.320
<v Steve Deline>That's not where people make their decisions about issues like this. People make their decisions about how they're going to vote on this at a gut level and at a visceral level and an emotional level.
<v Steve Deline>人们不是在那里对这样的议题做出决定的。人们在直觉、本能和情感层面上决定如何投票。

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:52.940
<v Ira Glass>This is what they learned-- to stop telling people things. The most important thing the canvasser should do is listen.
<v Ira Glass>这就是他们学到的——停止告诉人们事情。拉票员应该做的最重要的事情是倾听。

00:07:52.940 --> 00:08:13.940
<v Steve Deline>I think the big revelation was that our job was actually to go and give them the chance to talk about their own life, and realize that you know, maybe that led them to conclusions that were a little different than they'd thought.
<v Steve Deline>我认为最大的启示是，我们的工作实际上是去给他们机会谈论自己的生活，并意识到，也许这使他们得出了与他们原先想法略有不同的结论。

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:25.060
<v Ira Glass>So they're out doing this, trying their canvasses. And it took them less than a year to start to get decent results like you heard with that nurse. A small but noticeable number of people at the door told them that their minds had been changed.
<v Ira Glass>所以他们出去做这件事，尝试他们的拉票活动。不到一年，他们就开始获得像你从那位护士身上听到的那样不错的结果。门口一小部分但显眼的人告诉他们，他们的想法被改变了。

00:08:25.060 --> 00:09:00.770
<v Ira Glass>But of course, the only way to tell for sure if their minds changed and stayed changed was to have outsiders-- scientists-- do a proper case control study. And that's why the canvassers invited in two political scientists to do the original study that was published in Science magazine. That grad student from UCLA-- Michael LaCour-- did the actual research. But the study design and results were overseen by a big deal political scientist from Columbia University named Donald Green, who's kind of legendary in that world for transforming how people do field experiments. And Green says that before they started, he expected the results were going to show--
<v Ira Glass>当然，确定他们的想法是否改变并持续改变的唯一方法，是让局外人——科学家——进行适当的对照研究。这就是为什么拉票员邀请了两位政治学家来进行最初发表在《科学》杂志上的研究。那位加州大学洛杉矶分校的研究生——迈克尔·拉库尔——进行了实际研究。但研究设计和结果由哥伦比亚大学一位大人物政治学家唐纳德·格林监督，他在那个领域因彻底改变人们进行实地实验的方式而具有传奇色彩。格林说，在他们开始之前，他预计结果会显示——

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:06.110
<v Donald Green>Short-term effects. I thought that those effects would subside in a few days' time.
<v Donald Green>短期效应。我以为这些效应会在几天内消退。

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:14.640
<v Ira Glass>He thought that 'cause pretty much that's what always happens. People change their minds and then the effect wears off. Or much more common, they don't change their minds at all.
<v Ira Glass>他那样想是因为这几乎总是发生的情况。人们改变主意，然后效果就消失了。或者更常见的是，他们根本不会改变主意。

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:27.300
<v Ira Glass>There's a hefty body of research documenting something called the backfire effect, which happens with most of us. When we're confronted with evidence disproving something that we personally believe, most of us generally just dig in, and we believe it more.
<v Ira Glass>有大量的研究记录了一种被称为“逆火效应”的现象，这发生在我们大多数人身上。当我们面对反驳我们个人信仰的证据时，我们大多数人通常只是更加固执，我们更加相信它。

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:42.040
<v Ira Glass>This is a phenomenon, by the way, that's familiar to anybody who's ever been to a Thanksgiving dinner during an election year and tried to argue with some favorite uncle's wrongheaded political opinions. Never works, right? Facts mean nothing. And by the way, Uncle Lenny, I am not talking about you.
<v Ira Glass>顺便说一句，这种现象对于任何在选举年参加感恩节晚餐并试图与某个最喜爱的叔叔争论其错误政治观点的人来说都很熟悉。从来没用，对吧？事实毫无意义。顺便说一句，莱尼叔叔，我说的不是你。

00:09:42.040 --> 00:10:01.260
<v Ira Glass>But this is what was so revolutionary about the data that this grad student seemed to be gathering about these canvassers. He said he did online surveys with the voters who got visited by the canvassers and they showed that their minds changed and stayed changed for six months, nine months, a year after the canvasser knocked at the door.
<v Ira Glass>但这正是那个研究生似乎正在收集的关于这些拉票员的数据所具有的革命性意义。他说他对被拉票员访问过的选民进行了在线调查，结果显示他们的想法改变了，并且在拉票员敲门后的六个月、九个月、一年里一直保持改变。

00:10:01.260 --> 00:10:19.000
<v Ira Glass>Green told me that he and his colleagues had read through over 900 papers, never seen anything like this result, to change people's opinions and have it last like this. Though interestingly, when LaCour first showed his data to Professor Green, Green thought, this data is so perfect, these results might be real. But OK, best to be sure.
<v Ira Glass>格林告诉我，他和他的同事们阅读了900多篇论文，从未见过像这样的结果，即改变人们的观点并使之持续这么久。尽管有趣的是，当拉库尔第一次向格林教授展示他的数据时，格林认为，这些数据太完美了，这些结果可能是真的。但好的，最好确定一下。

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:26.710
<v Ira Glass>And he told LaCour to do the entire study-- canvasses, surveys, everything-- a second time. Which LaCour did. Same result.
<v Ira Glass>他告诉拉库尔把整个研究——拉票、调查、一切——再做一遍。拉库尔照做了。结果一样。

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:37.390
<v Ira Glass>Now, of course, later, Green's suspicions would be confirmed in spades. All that data would be shown to be fraudulent. But here's what LaCour said he found in the original study published in Science .
<v Ira Glass>当然，后来格林的怀疑被彻底证实了。所有这些数据都被证明是伪造的。但这是拉库尔在发表于《科学》杂志的原始研究中声称的发现。

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:56.360
<v Ira Glass>OK, so what I'm about to say is from the discredited results of a retracted study. This study was about gay marriage. It was about convincing people to support gay marriage. And the study claimed that a year after the canvassers visited voters' homes, the number of voters supporting gay marriage had jumped 15%. 15%.
<v Ira Glass>好了，我接下来要说的来自于一项被撤回的、被抹黑的研究结果。这项研究是关于同性婚姻的。它是关于说服人们支持同性婚姻。研究声称，在拉票员访问选民家庭一年后，支持同性婚姻的选民人数跃升了15%。15%。

00:10:56.360 --> 00:11:16.110
<v Ira Glass>But-- and this was key-- according to the study, the 15% increase only happened if the canvasser at the door had been gay and admitted being gay to the voter. People who talked to other canvassers only rose 3%, which is about as much as the nation as a whole changed on the issue of gay marriage that year.
<v Ira Glass>但是——这是关键——根据这项研究，只有当门口的拉票员是同性恋并向选民承认自己是同性恋时，15%的增长才会发生。与非同性恋拉票员交谈的人数仅上升了3%，这大约与那一年全美在同性婚姻议题上的变化程度相当。

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:44.010
<v Ira Glass>It was a similar effect with abortion, according LaCour. He said the number of people who became pro-choice after the canvass rose 5%. And again, the effect only lasted if the canvasser at the door revealed that she had had an abortion. It was talking to somebody affected by the issue that seemed to be changing people's minds.
<v Ira Glass>根据拉库尔的研究，堕胎方面也有类似的效果。他说，拉票后变成支持堕胎的人数上升了5%。同样，只有当门口的拉票员透露她曾经堕胎过，效果才会持续。似乎是与受此议题影响的人交谈改变了人们的想法。

00:11:44.010 --> 00:12:08.230
<v Ira Glass>I actually interviewed Michael LaCour a year ago for the story that I did for our show about all this. I have to say, it's strange listening to that interview today, knowing what happened just a few weeks after we talked, that his data would be shown to be fraudulent. 'Cause when I talked to him, he really talked like somebody doing a real study with real surveys. Like for instance, I asked him how attitudes towards abortion changed over time among the voters that he was studying.
<v Ira Glass>我实际上在一年前就我为我们的节目所做的关于这一切的报道采访了迈克尔·拉库尔。我必须说，今天听那次采访感觉很奇怪，因为我知道在我们谈话几周后发生的事情，他的数据被证明是欺诈性的。因为当我与他交谈时，他真的像是一个正在进行真实研究、有着真实调查的人。例如，我问他选民对堕胎的态度是如何随时间变化的。

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:24.600
<v Michael Lacour>So that's still ongoing. Because I'm tracking longitudinally these individuals over a course. I don't want to give out a hard number, but it stays relatively flat. And I think it levels off around five percentage points. That's around 200 days later.
<v Michael Lacour>那还在进行中。因为我正在对这些个体进行纵向追踪。我不想给出一个确切的数字，但它保持相对平稳。我认为在大约200天后趋于平稳，大约在5个百分点左右。

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:27.140
<v Ira Glass>He offered conclusions from his data. Like this one.
<v Ira Glass>他从他的数据中得出了结论。比如这个。

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:41.400
<v Michael Lacour>So one of the things is people change their-- when they had contact with a gay person discussing gay equality-- they changed their minds, not just about the policy issue about same sex marriage. They also changed their minds about gay people, gays and lesbians as a group.
<v Michael Lacour>所以其中一点是人们改变了他们的——当他们与一位讨论同性恋平权的同性恋者接触时——他们改变了想法，不仅是关于同性婚姻的政策议题。他们也改变了对同性恋者、作为一个群体的男同性恋和女同性恋的看法。

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:45.330
<v Ira Glass>Nice if that were true. If there were data supporting it.
<v Ira Glass>如果那是真的就好了。如果有支持它的数据的话。

00:12:45.330 --> 00:13:00.600
<v Ira Glass>Or there was this finding, which is so particular and specific that again, if there is no data supporting this, it's just a very idiosyncratic thing to decide to say is true. LaCour said that he graded each gay canvasser on how stereotypically gay they appeared. And--
<v Ira Glass>或者有这样一个发现，它是如此具体和特殊，以至于再次重申，如果没有支持它的数据，决定说它是真的只是一件非常独特的事情。拉库尔说他给每位同性恋拉票员打分，看他们看起来有多么刻板的同性恋倾向。而且——

00:13:00.600 --> 00:13:06.910
<v Michael Lacour>The gay canvassers who are perceived as straight had the largest effects.
<v Michael Lacour>那些被认为看起来像异性恋的同性恋拉票员产生了最大的影响。

00:13:06.910 --> 00:13:10.120
<v Ira Glass>That is, they were able to convince the most voters to support gay marriage.
<v Ira Glass>也就是说，他们能够说服最多的选民支持同性婚姻。

00:13:10.120 --> 00:13:25.070
<v Michael Lacour>So this suggests that voters may ordinarily have an unflattering mental image of a stereotypical gay person. And the discrepancy between the stereotype and the person standing on the doorstep creates a space for attitude change.
<v Michael Lacour>这表明，选民通常可能对典型的同性恋者有不太讨喜的心理形象。而刻板印象与站在门口的人之间的差异为态度的改变创造了空间。

00:13:25.070 --> 00:13:26.500
<v Ira Glass>Right. I see.
<v Ira Glass>对。我明白了。

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:31.820
<v Michael Lacour>Especially when the canvassers are building empathy through respectful two-way conversation.
<v Michael Lacour>特别是当拉票员通过尊重的双向谈话建立共鸣时。

00:13:31.820 --> 00:13:54.650
<v Ira Glass>This vision of the world is such a hopeful vision of how the world might work. The world LaCour fabricated is one where intolerant people hold inaccurate stereotypes in their heads and all it takes is an empathetic conversation, one sincere talk, 20 minutes long, with somebody from the stereotyped group and the scales fall from their eyes. They realize they were wrong all along.
<v Ira Glass>这种世界观是对世界如何运作的一种充满希望的愿景。拉库尔捏造的世界是一个不宽容的人脑中持有不准确的刻板印象，而只需要一次充满同理心的谈话，一次诚恳的对话，20分钟长，与来自刻板印象群体的人交谈，鳞片就会从他们眼中掉落。他们意识到自己一直都错了。

00:13:54.650 --> 00:14:09.760
<v Ira Glass>I like that world. I wish I lived in that world. Maybe we do live in that world for all we know. But if we do, there's no data proving it.
<v Ira Glass>我喜欢那个世界。我希望我住在那个世界。也许我们确实住在那个世界，据我们所知。但如果我们确实住在那里，没有数据证明它。

00:14:09.760 --> 00:14:13.200
<v Ira Glass>[PHONE RINGING]
<v Ira Glass>（电话铃声）

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:13.690
<v Donald Green>Hello?
<v Donald Green>喂？

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:15.270
<v Ira Glass>Hey, Don Green?
<v Ira Glass>嘿，唐·格林吗？

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:16.130
<v Donald Green>Yes.
<v Donald Green>是的。

00:14:16.130 --> 00:14:17.370
<v Ira Glass>Ira Glass.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:18.320
<v Donald Green>Hey, Ira.
<v Donald Green>嘿，艾拉。

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:38.820
<v Ira Glass>Last May, a month after we ran our story about LaCour's study, six months after he published that study in Science magazine, I called his co-author, Columbia professor Donald Green. Green had just reconsidered what was going on in the data LaCour had collected and had asked Science to retract their article, which the magazine did. He seemed understandably sobered and sad.
<v Ira Glass>去年五月，在我们播出关于拉库尔研究的报道一个月后，也就是他在《科学》杂志发表该研究六个月后，我给他的合著者、哥伦比亚大学教授唐纳德·格林打了电话。格林刚刚重新审视了拉库尔收集的数据中发生的事情，并要求《科学》杂志撤回他们的文章，该杂志照做了。他看起来可以理解地变得冷静而悲伤。

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:46.820
<v Donald Green>It was such a waste. Not just of my time, obviously, but no, the Center and the canvassers' time.
<v Donald Green>这真是浪费。不仅浪费了我的时间，显然，更浪费了中心和拉票员的时间。

00:14:46.820 --> 00:14:47.180
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:48.660
<v Donald Green>It's just crazy.
<v Donald Green>这太疯狂了。

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:58.540
<v Ira Glass>The way the fraud was discovered was actually a little detective story of its own. A detective story that began kind of exactly the way they tell you in school that the scientific method is supposed to work.
<v Ira Glass>发现欺诈的方式实际上本身就是一个小侦探故事。一个侦探故事，开始的方式和学校里教你的科学方法应该运作的方式几乎一模一样。

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:13.560
<v Ira Glass>Two young researchers-- David Broockman and Joshua Kalla, both in their 20s-- tried to replicate what LaCour did. The  team from the Los Angeles LGBT Center and a Miami LGBT group was organizing a new canvass operation in Miami. Broockman and Kalla would study that.
<v Ira Glass>两名年轻的研究人员——戴维·布鲁克曼和约书亚·卡拉，都才20多岁——试图复制拉库尔所做的事情。洛杉矶LGBT中心的团队和一个迈阿密的LGBT组织正在迈阿密组织一项新的拉票行动。布鲁克曼和卡拉将研究这一点。

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:20.210
<v Ira Glass>Broockman was already suspicious of LaCour's data. It seemed too perfect. Then they started their research and they noticed something funny.
<v Ira Glass>布鲁克曼已经对拉库尔的数据产生了怀疑。它看起来太完美了。然后他们开始研究，发现了一些奇怪的事情。

00:15:20.210 --> 00:15:40.970
<v Ira Glass>The way this experiment works, like I said, the researchers do online surveys of the voters' opinions before and after the canvassers come to their doors and talk to them. And the researchers pay the voters to fill out those online surveys to motivate them to do the surveys. And tons of voters filled out LaCour's surveys, but not Broockman and Kalla's.
<v Ira Glass>正如我所说，这个实验的运作方式是，研究人员在拉票员来到选民门口并与他们交谈前后，对选民的观点进行在线调查。研究人员付费给选民以完成这些在线调查，从而激励他们参与。大量的选民完成了拉库尔的调查，但没有完成布鲁克曼和卡拉的调查。

00:15:40.970 --> 00:15:48.850
<v Ira Glass>This made no sense at all. And they didn't know what to do. If people didn't fill out their surveys, they'd have no study. They started to panic.
<v Ira Glass>这完全没有道理。他们不知道该怎么办。如果人们不完成他们的调查，他们就不会有研究。他们开始恐慌。

00:15:48.850 --> 00:16:10.300
<v Ira Glass>So they reached out to the survey company that LaCour said he used to find out exactly how LaCour got so many people to fill in their surveys. The company informed them that LaCour was not one of their clients, that the employee LaCour said he worked with did not exist, and in fact, this company did not have the capability to do the things LaCour said they did for him.
<v Ira Glass>所以他们联系了拉库尔声称使用的调查公司，以查清拉库尔到底是如何让那么多人完成他的调查的。该公司通知他们，拉库尔不是他们的客户，拉库尔声称与他合作的员工根本不存在，事实上，这家公司也没有能力做拉库尔声称他们为他做的事情。

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:25.750
<v Ira Glass>The hundreds of thousands of dollars that was supposedly paid out to survey responders, which LaCour said came from funders like the Ford Foundation-- who LaCour thanked in his study-- never was actually granted to LaCour. Given all that, it was hard to see how any of the data could be real.
<v Ira Glass>据称支付给调查受访者的数十万美元，拉库尔说这来自福特基金会等资助者——拉库尔在研究中感谢了他们——实际上从未拨给拉库尔。鉴于这一切，很难看出任何数据可能是真实的。

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:40.030
<v Ira Glass>But LaCour held firm. Said the data was real. He even wrote a paper defending it. But he said that he could not show the data to anybody because he had destroyed all the original surveys to protect the privacy of the respondents.
<v Ira Glass>但拉库尔坚持己见。说数据是真实的。他甚至写了一篇论文为之辩护。但他说他不能向任何人展示数据，因为他为了保护受访者的隐私，销毁了所有原始调查。

00:16:40.030 --> 00:16:55.330
<v Ira Glass>I'm not sure anybody believed him. He'd been headed for a prestigious job at Princeton University after finishing his doctoral thesis. Now that job evaporated. His collaborator Don Green says the odd thing was the fakery went way beyond what seemed to be necessary.
<v Ira Glass>我不确定是否有人相信他。他在完成博士论文后原本要前往普林斯顿大学担任一份令人羡慕的工作。现在那份工作化为乌有了。他的合作者唐·格林说，奇怪的是，造假程度远远超过了似乎必要的范围。

00:16:55.330 --> 00:17:10.900
<v Donald Green>And this was, I think, the thing that I just want to convey somehow, is that there was an incredible mountain of fabrication. It was the most baroque and ornate ornamentation.
<v Donald Green>我认为我想传达的一件事是，这里有一座令人难以置信的造假大山。它是最巴洛克和华丽的装饰。

00:17:10.900 --> 00:17:16.420
<v Donald Green>There were stories. There were anecdotes. My Dropbox is filled with graphs and charts.
<v Donald Green>有故事。有轶事。我的Dropbox里装满了图表。

00:17:16.420 --> 00:17:22.160
<v Ira Glass>What do you mean there's stories and anecdotes? I thought the only thing that was fabricated was the online surveys?
<v Ira Glass>你说的故事和轶事是什么意思？我以为唯一被捏造的是在线调查？

00:17:22.160 --> 00:17:33.130
<v Donald Green>Right. But there would be stories out of the survey. So he would say things like, "well did you know that some of the people in the survey came out?"
<v Donald Green>没错。但调查中会有故事。所以他会说诸如，“嗯，你知道调查中的一些人出柜了吗？”

00:17:33.130 --> 00:17:44.330
<v Ira Glass>As if, in later surveys, their attitudes about homosexuality had changed enough that they were comfortable finally admitting to the surveyors that they were gay. Except of course, the surveys didn't seem to exist.
<v Ira Glass>好像在后来的调查中，他们对同性恋的态度发生了足够的改变，以至于他们终于觉得舒服到向调查员承认自己是同性恋了。除了当然，这些调查似乎并不存在。

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:54.700
<v Donald Green>It's layer after layer of embellishment that the sheer magnitude of it makes it very hard to entertain the possibility that it's all made up.
<v Donald Green>这是一层又一层的装饰，其巨大的规模使得很难相信这完全是捏造的。

00:17:54.700 --> 00:18:06.770
<v Ira Glass>The day after the truth came to light, one of the guys who created the canvasses, Dave Fleischer, told me that that's what he found himself trying to get his head around, the layers of embellishment. He told me this story about working with Michael LaCour.
<v Ira Glass>真相大白后的第二天，创造这些拉票活动的其中一人，戴夫·弗莱彻，告诉我，这正是他发现自己试图理解的事情，那些装饰的层面。他告诉我这个关于与迈克尔·拉库尔合作的故事。

00:18:06.770 --> 00:18:30.050
<v Dave Fleischer>At our very first canvass where Mike was measuring us, right, we were running the canvass training, we're sending people out canvassing, we're going out canvassing. And Mike has a laptop set up. And as voters that we've talked to then fill out their first post-canvassing survey--
<v Dave Fleischer>在我们迈克测量我们的第一次拉票活动中，对吧，我们正在进行拉票培训，我们在派人出去拉票，我们自己也在拉票。迈克设置了一台笔记本电脑。当我们需要交谈的选民填写他们第一次拉票后的调查时——

00:18:30.050 --> 00:18:38.970
<v Ira Glass>In other words, after real life canvassers came to their doors, Michael LaCour is supposedly having these voters fill out online surveys. And he's telling the canvassers, those surveys--
<v Ira Glass>换句话说，在现实生活中的拉票员来到他们家门口后，迈克尔·拉库尔据称让这些选民填写在线调查。他告诉拉票员，那些调查——

00:18:38.970 --> 00:18:58.980
<v Dave Fleischer>Some of them are starting to trickle in. And Mike's got his laptop set up with him gazing intently at it. And his group of about a half dozen research assistants clustered around him gazing at it raptly, the way you would at election results on a presidential election night.
<v Dave Fleischer>其中一些开始陆续进来了。迈克全神贯注地盯着他的笔记本电脑。他的一组大约六名研究助理聚集在他周围，专注地盯着它，就像你在总统选举之夜看着选举结果那样。

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:23.770
<v Dave Fleischer>And after we finished the canvass de-brief and the volunteers leave, Mike invites us-- the team of staff organizers-- to come over and also glimpse the findings that are starting to emerge. And I really have thought back on this moment obviously, in the last 24 hours. Because it really looked like, oh my god, the results are coming in.
<v Dave Fleischer>在我们结束拉票汇报并且志愿者离开后，迈克邀请我们——工作人员团队——过去一瞥开始显现的调查结果。显然，我在过去24小时里真的回想起了这一刻。因为它看起来真的很像，天哪，结果出来了。

00:19:23.770 --> 00:19:28.210
<v Ira Glass>Wait, wait, wait. And so what was on his screen? Did you see his screen?
<v Ira Glass>等等，等等。那么他的屏幕上是什么？你看到他的屏幕了吗？

00:19:28.210 --> 00:19:45.920
<v Dave Fleischer>Oh yeah. It was like a-- heck, it's like data points coming in. And he would go back and forth between these two-- it's probably an old fashioned term from when I studied sociology-- but scattergrams.
<v Dave Fleischer>哦，是的。就像……天哪，就像数据点进来了。他会在两者之间来回切换——这可能是一个我学习社会学时的老派术语——但散点图。

00:19:45.920 --> 00:19:47.680
<v Ira Glass>These are like charts.
<v Ira Glass>这些就像图表。

00:19:47.680 --> 00:19:49.930
<v Dave Fleischer>Yes. There's a graphic depiction.
<v Dave Fleischer>是的。有一个图形化的描绘。

00:19:49.930 --> 00:19:56.030
<v Ira Glass>And so he's going back and forth. And are those charts changing over the course of the time you look at them?
<v Ira Glass>所以他在来回切换。而且那些图表在你看着它们的过程中是在变化的吗？

00:19:56.030 --> 00:19:57.310
<v Dave Fleischer>Oh yeah.
<v Dave Fleischer>哦，是的。

00:19:57.310 --> 00:19:59.340
<v Ira Glass>It's so elaborate.
<v Ira Glass>太复杂了。

00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:04.300
<v Dave Fleischer>Well, yes. It would have been a whole lot easier just to have measured our work.
<v Dave Fleischer>嗯，是的。如果只是衡量我们的工作会容易得多。

00:20:04.300 --> 00:20:05.300
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>（笑）

00:20:05.300 --> 00:20:14.730
<v Dave Fleischer>[LAUGHS]
<v Dave Fleischer>（笑）

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:22.210
<v Ira Glass>How do you explain this? Like, you worked with him for many, many hours. How are you putting this together in your head?
<v Ira Glass>你怎么解释这个？你和他共事了许多许多小时。你在脑海里是怎么把这些拼凑起来的？

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:40.620
<v Dave Fleischer>Two years, Ira. And yeah, it is not really understandable to me. Because you spoke, I believe, with Mike on the phone.
<v Dave Fleischer>两年，艾拉。是的，这对我来说真的无法理解。因为我相信你和迈克通过电话。

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:41.000
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是的。

00:20:41.000 --> 00:21:10.910
<v Dave Fleischer>And he's a smart guy. He, I think, is genuinely a caring guy. He genuinely loves statistics. But there is a piece of this that is incomprehensible when somebody who has so much makes such a grievous choice.
<v Dave Fleischer>他是一个聪明人，我认为他确实是一个关心他人的家伙。他真心热爱统计学。但当一个拥有那么多的人做出如此严重的错误选择时，这其中有一部分是令人费解的。

00:21:10.910 --> 00:21:37.000
<v Ira Glass>Canvass organizers say staff and 73 volunteers worked for more than 3,600 hours, including knocking on doors and talking to over 400 voters for the fake study. Since all this went down last year, Michael LaCour still has not admitted to fabricating data in public or in private with the colleagues of his that I've spoken to. He declined my invitation to come on the radio this week and talk about this. He declined to say whether the data was real or fake.
<v Ira Glass>拉票组织者说，工作人员和73名志愿者为这项假研究工作了超过3600小时，包括敲门并与400多名选民交谈。自去年这一切发生以来，迈克尔·拉库尔仍然没有公开或私下向我交谈过的他的同事承认捏造数据。他拒绝了我本周邀请他上广播谈论此事的邀请。他拒绝说明数据是真实的还是虚假的。

00:21:37.000 --> 00:21:59.980
<v Ira Glass>The one chink in this position came last month, when out of the blue he texted Dave Fleischer. "Hi Dave. If you're willing, I'd like to meet with you face to face. To apologize." Dave told me he hasn't been able to bring himself to respond just yet. He's not ready.
<v Ira Glass>在这个立场上的唯一裂缝出现在上个月，当时他突然给戴夫·弗莱彻发了短信。“嗨，戴夫。如果你愿意的话，我想和你面对面谈谈。道歉。”戴夫告诉我，他至今还没能让自己回复。他还没准备好。

00:21:59.980 --> 00:22:10.340
<v David Broockman>Yeah, so what we've done so far is the Miami work. I think that's what we're ready to show you some of the early sneak peek into. I think you're the first person outside of the study--
<v David Broockman>是的，所以到目前为止我们已经完成了迈阿密的工作。我想那就是我们准备向你展示一些初步预览的东西。我想你是研究之外的第一个人——

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:25.420
<v Ira Glass>That's David Broockman, one of the guys who discovered the fraud in the original study, and the co-author of the new study with real surveys and real data that was published this week in Science magazine. This recording has him talking to me last August, three months after Science retracted the original study.
<v Ira Glass>那是戴维·布鲁克曼，最初那项研究中发现欺诈的人之一，也是本周发表在《科学》杂志上的、有着真实调查和真实数据的这项新研究的合著者。这段录音是他去年八月，即《科学》杂志撤回原始研究三个月后与我的谈话。

00:22:25.420 --> 00:22:34.820
<v Ira Glass>It was Broockman and his research partner remember, who discovered LaCour's fraud when they tried to replicate his methods in Miami. Now the Miami data was in, the first part of it anyway.
<v Ira Glass>还记得是布鲁克曼和他的研究伙伴发现了拉库尔的欺诈行为，当时他们试图在迈阿密复制他的方法。现在迈阿密的有数据了，至少是第一部分。

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:55.330
<v Ira Glass>The canvassers in Miami went door to door trying to change voters' attitudes about transgendered people. Miami-Dade County passed an ordinance in 2014 banning discrimination against transgender people. And these canvassers were trying to convince voters that that was a good idea, and to support it if a ballot measure someday tried to strike it down. And they succeeded.
<v Ira Glass>迈阿密的拉票员挨家挨户尝试改变选民对跨性别者的态度。迈阿密-戴德县在2014年通过了一项法令，禁止歧视跨性别者。这些拉票员试图说服选民这是一个好主意，并支持它，如果将来有投票倡议试图废除它的话。他们成功了。

00:22:55.330 --> 00:23:16.670
<v David Broockman>And you see just a huge increase in the acceptance towards transgender people. And overall, what you see is it's basically the largest effect I've ever seen for any political tactic. So much more than phone calls or mail or other forms of canvassing that I've seen. The effect is just huge.
<v David Broockman>你会看到对跨性别者的接纳程度有巨大的提升。总的来说，你看到的是我见过的任何政治策略中最大的效果。远比我见过的电话、邮件或其他形式的拉票效果好得多。效果就是这么巨大。

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:26.710
<v Ira Glass>In other words, the thing that was so exciting about the discredited study turned out to be true. These canvasses can change people's minds. For real.
<v Ira Glass>换句话说，那项被抹黑研究中令人兴奋的事情被证明是真的。这些拉票活动确实可以改变人们的想法。真的。

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:55.810
<v Ira Glass>Roughly one out of 10 voters changed their attitudes about transgender people. As the study notes, it took from 1998 to 2012-- 14 years-- for the country as a whole to have a comparable change in its attitudes towards gay people. It only took the canvassers 20 minutes or less to do this for trans people. Broockman said the only political tactic that has ever been shown to be more effective than this in affecting attitudes is personally meeting somebody who's running for office, which of course, is a very different kind of thing.
<v Ira Glass>大约十分之一的选民改变了他们对跨性别者的态度。正如研究指出的，国家整体对同性恋者的态度发生相当程度的改变花费了1998年到2012年——14年的时间。拉票员只用了20分钟或更短的时间就为跨性别者做到了这一点。布鲁克曼说，唯一被证明在影响态度方面比这更有效的政治策略是亲自见到竞选公职的人，这当然是一种非常不同的事情。

00:23:55.810 --> 00:24:12.590
<v David Broockman>More impressive is that the effect appears to last, that you basically see the exact same pattern of results six weeks later. We're going to start measuring again at, I think we decided three months and six months. So who knows if these will continue to last into the future?
<v David Broockman>更令人印象深刻的是，这种效果似乎会持续，你基本上在六周后看到了完全相同的模式结果。我想我们决定在三个月和六个月时再次开始测量。所以谁知道这些是否会持续到未来呢？

00:24:12.590 --> 00:24:19.280
<v Ira Glass>They did. Three months later, attitudes stayed changed. Again, same as the discredited study.
<v Ira Glass>确实持续了。三个月后，态度保持改变。同样，和那项被抹黑的研究一样。

00:24:19.280 --> 00:24:39.700
<v Ira Glass>But where it gets really interesting is the stuff that the new guys found that is different from the original study. First difference? Remember how the original study said that only a gay canvasser could convince a voter to support gay marriage? And only a woman who talked about her own abortion could convince a voter to become pro-choice? David Broockman says on these trans issues--
<v Ira Glass>但最有趣的地方在于新团队发现的与原始研究不同的东西。第一个不同点？还记得原始研究说只有同性恋拉票员才能说服选民支持同性婚姻吗？只有谈论自己堕胎经历的女性才能说服选民成为支持堕胎权的人吗？戴维·布鲁克曼说，在这些跨性别议题上——

00:24:39.700 --> 00:24:47.460
<v David Broockman>That's not what we're finding at all. We see that both transgender and non-transgender canvassers are effective, and they're similarly effective.
<v David Broockman>那根本不是我们发现的情况。我们看到跨性别和非跨性别拉票员都很有效，而且他们的效果相似。

00:24:47.460 --> 00:25:05.280
<v Ira Glass>Which is a profoundly different finding from the original discredited study. The original study seemed to say that it was meeting somebody, relating to a gay person or a trans person or whoever that changed people's attitudes. It was empathy that did the trick. But this new study says it's something else entirely.
<v Ira Glass>这是一个与最初被抹黑的研究极其不同的发现。原始研究似乎说，是遇见某人、与同性恋者或跨性别者或任何人产生关联改变了人们的态度。是同理心起了作用。但这项新研究说那是其他完全不同的东西。

00:25:05.280 --> 00:25:17.360
<v David Broockman>Really what's happening here is it's listening and asking questions to voters to get them to say the words and to get them to do the mental work to think through how the experiences they have had are relevant to this issue.
<v David Broockman>这里真正发生的是倾听并向选民提问，让他们说出那些话，并进行脑力劳动，思考他们拥有的经历与这个问题有何关联。

00:25:17.360 --> 00:25:20.600
<v Ira Glass>In other words, there's this ideal of well, if you just met a gay person--
<v Ira Glass>换句话说，有一种理想，好吧，如果你只是遇见了一个同性恋者——

00:25:20.600 --> 00:25:20.880
<v David Broockman>That's right.
<v David Broockman>那是对的。

00:25:20.880 --> 00:25:23.330
<v Ira Glass>Then you'd have this feeling. You're saying nah, that's not it.
<v Ira Glass>那么你就会有这种感觉。你说，不，不是那样的。

00:25:23.330 --> 00:25:31.620
<v David Broockman>The idea is that if the words come out of your own mouth, it's much more meaningful to you than if they come out of somebody else's mouth.
<v David Broockman>想法是，如果这些话出自你自己的口，它对你来说比出自别人的口要有意义得多。

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:34.240
<v Ira Glass>That's so funny, 'cause like, who do you believe? Oh, I believe myself.
<v Ira Glass>这太搞笑了，因为，你相信谁？哦，我相信我自己。

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:39.200
<v David Broockman>Exactly. So it's not that this isn't common sense. But it's also not what any campaigns do.
<v David Broockman>完全正确。所以这并不是说这不是常识。但这也并非任何竞选活动所做的。

00:25:39.200 --> 00:25:56.130
<v Ira Glass>Broockman and his co-author have also been working on a study of the abortion canvasses organized by the Los Angeles LGBT Center. And here's another difference from the original discredited research done by Michael LaCour. Broockman has found the abortion canvasses failed. They didn't actually convince people.
<v Ira Glass>布鲁克曼和他的合著者也一直在研究洛杉矶LGBT中心组织的堕胎拉票活动。这里是与迈克尔·拉库尔做的最初那项被抹黑的研究的另一个区别。布鲁克曼发现堕胎拉票活动失败了。他们并没有真正说服任何人。

00:25:56.130 --> 00:25:56.630
<v David Broockman>Zero.
<v David Broockman>零。

00:25:56.630 --> 00:25:57.800
<v Ira Glass>No effect.
<v Ira Glass>没有效果。

00:25:57.800 --> 00:25:58.930
<v David Broockman>No effect for anybody.
<v David Broockman>对任何人都没有效果。

00:25:58.930 --> 00:26:00.300
<v Ira Glass>No effect for anyone.
<v Ira Glass>对任何人都没有效果。

00:26:00.300 --> 00:26:01.780
<v David Broockman>Right.
<v David Broockman>对。

00:26:01.780 --> 00:26:08.310
<v Ira Glass>How do you explain that this worked with the trans issue, but it didn't work with the abortion issue?
<v Ira Glass>你如何解释这对跨性别议题有效，但对堕胎议题无效？

00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:19.130
<v David Broockman>Well, I think there's a lot of possibilities. One possibility is that trans issues are so new, whereas abortion is so ingrained that it was just easier. Like--
<v David Broockman>嗯，我认为有很多可能性。一种可能是跨性别议题太新了，而堕胎是根深蒂固的，所以更容易。就像——

00:26:19.130 --> 00:26:22.590
<v Ira Glass>In other words, people haven't really formed very firm opinions about trans issues--
<v Ira Glass>换句话说，人们还没有真正就跨性别议题形成非常坚定的观点——

00:26:22.590 --> 00:26:23.010
<v David Broockman>Exactly.
<v David Broockman>完全正确。

00:26:23.010 --> 00:26:27.420
<v Ira Glass>Whereas everybody knows where they stand. Every adult knows whether they're for or against abortion rights.
<v Ira Glass>而每个人都知道他们的立场。每个成年人都知道他们是支持还是反对堕胎权。

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:31.680
<v David Broockman>Right. So I think that's possible.
<v David Broockman>对。所以我认为这是可能的。

00:26:31.680 --> 00:26:54.980
<v Ira Glass>The other possibility, of course, is that canvass organizers just haven't figured out yet how to talk about abortion at the doorstep in a way that lastingly convinces anyone. We tried very hard, by the way. Somebody actually flew to Los Angeles to locate that nurse that you heard at the beginning of the program to find out if she went back to her old pro-life beliefs after the canvasser walked away from the door. But we couldn't find her.
<v Ira Glass>另一种可能性，当然，是拉票组织者还没有找到在门口以一种能够持续说服任何人的方式谈论堕胎的方法。顺便说一下，我们尝试得非常努力。有人甚至飞往洛杉矶寻找你在节目开头听到的那位护士，看看她在拉票员离开门口后是否回到了她原来反堕胎的信仰。但我们没能找到她。

00:26:54.980 --> 00:27:23.440
<v Ira Glass>I will say in videos of the Miami canvass, one thing that's really striking is how malleable people do seem to be on the transgender issues in this way that's very hard to imagine on abortion rights. Sometimes a canvasser has to explain to the voters exactly what transgender means. They have to show videos to be sure that it's understood what it is that they're even talking about. Or take this man, an older voter with a kind face who stands at his doorstep in a sleeveless undershirt telling that same canvasser that you heard at the beginning of the program--
<v Ira Glass>我想说的是，在迈阿密拉票活动的视频中，有一点非常引人注目的是，人们在跨性别议题上看起来确实很易受影响，这在堕胎权上很难想象。有时拉票员必须向选民确切解释跨性别意味着什么。他们必须展示视频以确保对方理解他们到底在谈论什么。或者看这位男士，一位面容和善的老年选民，他站在门口穿着无袖背心，告诉你在节目开头听到的那位拉票员——

00:27:23.440 --> 00:27:41.500
<v Male Voter>There is one thing that disturb me. I don't know if let's say, a man that is a fag using a man's clothes going into ladies bathroom. That I don't really like.
<v Male Voter>有一件事困扰我。我不知道，如果说，一个男人是个男同性恋，穿着男人的衣服进入女士洗手间。我不喜欢那样。

00:27:41.500 --> 00:27:43.570
<v Canvasser>Mhm. Did you say a man who is a--
<v Canvasser>嗯。你是说一个男人是——

00:27:43.570 --> 00:27:49.740
<v Ira Glass>They talked for a while. He's from Ecuador. Doesn't get out much because he's at home caring for his wife, who has dementia.
<v Ira Glass>他们谈了很久。他来自厄瓜多尔。不怎么出门，因为他在家照顾患有痴呆症的妻子。

00:27:49.740 --> 00:28:00.040
<v Ira Glass>Doesn't have many friends. And seems genuinely confused about the difference between trans and gay. The canvasser explains what is what about that to him. She even helpfully informs him--
<v Ira Glass>没有多少朋友。看起来真的对跨性别和同性恋的区别感到困惑。拉票员向他解释了关于那件事的一切。她甚至很有帮助地告诉他——

00:28:00.040 --> 00:28:02.770
<v Canvasser>So we don't usually use the word fag. So--
<v Canvasser>所以我们通常不会用那个词（对同性恋的蔑称）。所以——

00:28:02.770 --> 00:28:03.740
<v Male Voter>Well, it's--I--
<v Male Voter>嗯，它是——我——

00:28:03.740 --> 00:28:04.710
<v Canvasser>[LAUGHS]
<v Canvasser>（笑）

00:28:04.710 --> 00:28:19.240
<v Ira Glass>He apologizes. Says that spending all of his time with his wife, maybe he's getting demented too. Forgetting how to talk to people. And by the end, it is just like the researcher Broockman said. The voter is the one reflecting on his experience, drawing new conclusions.
<v Ira Glass>他道歉了。说他把所有时间都用来陪妻子，也许他自己也变得痴呆了。忘了怎么和人交流。到最后，这就像研究员布鲁克曼说的那样。选民自己是在反思他的经历，得出新的结论。

00:28:19.240 --> 00:28:20.190
<v Canvasser>[LAUGHS] So--
<v Canvasser>（笑）所以——

00:28:20.190 --> 00:28:28.390
<v Male Voter>I'm so glad that I'm talking to a really intelligent person that made me--
<v Male Voter>我很高兴我正在和一个非常聪明的人交谈，这让我——

00:28:28.390 --> 00:28:35.220
<v Ira Glass>It's kind of hard to hear. He's saying, I'm glad to be talking to an intelligent person that made me think about my own background. And that it was very old.
<v Ira Glass>这有点难听清。他说，我很高兴能和一个聪明人交谈，这让我思考我自己的背景。而且那是非常古老的。

00:28:35.220 --> 00:28:38.430
<v Male Voter>Listen, probably I was mistaken.
<v Male Voter>听着，可能我错了。

00:28:38.430 --> 00:28:48.780
<v Ira Glass>Listen, probably I was mistaken.
<v Ira Glass>听着，可能我错了。

00:28:48.780 --> 00:28:56.780
<v Ira Glass>OK, one last thing. I guess this is the most obvious question probably that I could ask in this story. And I just want you to know I did ask it. Here's the recording.
<v Ira Glass>好的，最后一件事情。我想这可能是我在这个故事中能问的最明显的问题。我只是想让你知道我确实问了。这是录音。

00:28:56.780 --> 00:29:07.860
<v Ira Glass>OK, so there's one study published in Science magazine like a year ago. It says that this works. It was proven to be a fraud. Now you guys are coming out with a study in Science magazine saying that this works. Why should we believe you?
<v Ira Glass>好的，一年前《科学》杂志上发表了一项研究。它说这有效。它被证明是一场骗局。现在你们在《科学》杂志上发表了一项研究说这有效。我们为什么要相信你们？

00:29:07.860 --> 00:29:11.030
<v David Broockman>[LAUGHS]
<v David Broockman>（笑）

00:29:11.030 --> 00:29:31.880
<v David Broockman>We've thought about this. As silly as it was, we did have one of our old graduate advisors-- Gabriel Lenz at Berkeley-- independently verify and go through that the original raw data collected in the survey platform matched all the stages of the data that we collected, data from the study that we'll then post publicly.
