00:54:29.940 --> 00:54:44.840
<v Christopher Alesevich>I looked at these special scissors, and they were very dull. They had rounded tips, and so they were not sharp, as where my previous lesson was that sharp scissors are dangerous. And so therefore, not-sharp scissors are not dangerous.
<v Christopher Alesevich>我看了这些特殊的剪刀，它们非常钝。它们有圆形的尖端，所以它们不尖锐，正如我之前的教训是尖锐的剪刀是危险的。因此，不尖锐的剪刀是不危险的。

00:54:44.840 --> 00:54:45.680
<v Brian Reed>How old were you?
<v Brian Reed>你当时多大？

00:54:45.680 --> 00:54:47.770
<v Christopher Alesevich>So this was second grade. I must have been--
<v Christopher Alesevich>所以这是二年级。我一定——

00:54:47.770 --> 00:54:50.450
<v Chris's Mother>You were probably seven. But you're a January birthday, so--
<v Chris's Mother>你可能七岁。但你是1月的生日，所以——

00:54:50.450 --> 00:55:05.570
<v Christopher Alesevich>So not only was I so sure that scissors that didn't look sharp were not dangerous, but then I had to prove to another person my knowledge of this matter and to show off that hey, you're wrong. These scissors are not dangerous.
<v Christopher Alesevich>所以我不但那么确定看起来不尖锐的剪刀是不危险的，而且我还必须向另一个人证明我对此事的了解，并炫耀嘿，你错了。这些剪刀不危险。

00:55:05.570 --> 00:55:22.300
<v Brian Reed>It's amazing that the very serious lesson you'd been taught by being hospitalized a couple years earlier, the lesson you'd been taught to specifically watch yourself around scissors was the thing that inspired you to be extra braggadocious--
<v Brian Reed>令人惊奇的是，通过几年前住院你被教导的非常严肃的教训，你被教导要特别注意剪刀的教训，正是激发你变得额外狂妄——

00:55:22.300 --> 00:55:23.020
<v Christopher Alesevich>Confident. That's right.
<v Christopher Alesevich>自信。就是那样。

00:55:23.020 --> 00:55:24.490
<v Brian Reed>--and careless around scissors.
<v Brian Reed>——和在剪刀周围粗心大意的东西。

00:55:24.490 --> 00:55:25.920
<v Chris's Mother>Yes. Yes.
<v Chris's Mother>是的。是的。

00:55:25.920 --> 00:55:32.280
<v Christopher Alesevich>So what I did, I stuck out my hand and my finger, and I was like, here cut. It's not going to cut.
<v Christopher Alesevich>所以我做了什么，我伸出了我的手和手指，我就像，来剪。它不会剪断的。

00:55:32.280 --> 00:55:33.130
<v Brian Reed>[LAUGHS] God.
<v Brian Reed>（笑）上帝。

00:55:33.130 --> 00:55:37.650
<v Christopher Alesevich>Lo and behold, it cut my finger. I was like, "oh, crap!"
<v Christopher Alesevich>结果，它剪断了我的手指。我就像，“噢，糟了！”

00:55:37.650 --> 00:55:38.140
<v Chris's Mother>[LAUGHS]
<v Chris's Mother>（笑）

00:55:38.140 --> 00:55:39.110
<v Christopher Alesevich>[LAUGHS]
<v Christopher Alesevich>（笑）

00:55:39.110 --> 00:55:50.910
<v Brian Reed>So I guess his parents had to get called by the principal. He couldn't go home on the bus 'cause he was bleeding quite profusely. Again, his parents had to be told, "Christopher has had an incident with scissors." [LAUGHS]
<v Brian Reed>所以我猜他的父母不得不被校长叫来。他不能坐校车回家，因为他在大量流血。再次，他的父母不得不被告知，“克里斯托弗又发生了一起剪刀事件。”（笑）

00:55:50.910 --> 00:55:54.220
<v Brian Reed>He was fine. But you know.
<v Brian Reed>他没事。但你知道的。

00:55:54.220 --> 00:55:58.090
<v Brian Reed>So what was the lesson of that accident?
<v Brian Reed>所以那次事故的教训是什么？

00:55:58.090 --> 00:56:04.750
<v Christopher Alesevich>So the lesson of that accident is that all scissors are sharp, regardless of whether they look sharp or not.
<v Christopher Alesevich>所以那次事故的教训是所有的剪刀都是尖锐的，无论它们看起来尖锐还是不尖锐。

00:56:04.750 --> 00:56:05.590
<v Brian Reed>[LAUGHS]
<v Brian Reed>（笑）

00:56:05.590 --> 00:56:12.440
<v Christopher Alesevich>And actually I did internalize that lesson. And I'd like to point out, there has not been a third scissors incident.
<v Christopher Alesevich>事实上我确实内化了那个教训。我想指出，没有发生第三次剪刀事件。

00:56:12.440 --> 00:56:14.580
<v Brian Reed>And a good 25 years have gone by.
<v Brian Reed>而且过去了整整25年。

00:56:14.580 --> 00:56:15.020
<v Chris's Mother>Mhm.
<v Chris's Mother>嗯哼。

00:56:15.020 --> 00:56:20.750
<v Christopher Alesevich>Yeah. Lesson learned there.
<v Christopher Alesevich>是的。在那里学到了教训。

00:56:20.750 --> 00:56:22.580
<v Ira Glass>Brian Reed, thank you very much.
<v Ira Glass>布莱恩·里德，非常感谢你。

00:56:22.580 --> 00:56:23.700
<v Brian Reed>Of course. Any time.
<v Brian Reed>当然。随时。

00:56:23.700 --> 00:57:15.240
<v Ira Glass>Always a fine report. Brian Reed is the senior producer of our program.
<v Ira Glass>总是很好的报告。布莱恩·里德是我们节目的高级制片人。

00:57:15.240 --> 00:57:28.810
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Stephanie Foo, Zoe Chace, Sean Cole, Neil Drumming, Chana Joffe-Walt, Miki Meek, Jonathan Menjivar, Robyn Semien, Alissa Shipp, and Nancy Updike. Our senior producer is Brian Reed. Our editor is Joel Lovell. Julie Snyder's our editorial consultant. Our technical director is Matt Tierney.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由斯蒂芬妮·福、佐伊·蔡斯、肖恩·科尔、尼尔·德鲁明、查娜·乔夫-沃尔特、米基·米克、乔纳森·门吉瓦尔、罗宾·塞米恩、阿丽莎·希普和南希·厄普代克制作。我们的高级制片人是布莱恩·里德。我们的编辑是乔尔·洛弗尔。朱莉·斯奈德是我们的编辑顾问。我们的技术总监是马特·蒂尔尼。

00:57:28.810 --> 00:57:39.910
<v Ira Glass>Production help from Lyra Smith. Special thanks today to Shane Fletcher. Our website, thisamericanlife.org. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange.
<v Ira Glass>制作帮助来自莱拉·史密斯。今天特别感谢肖恩·弗莱彻。我们的网站，thisamericanlife.org。《美国生活》由PRX，公共广播交易所交付给公共广播电台。

00:57:39.910 --> 00:57:49.040
<v Ira Glass>Thanks as always to our program's co-founder, Mr. Torey Malatia. You know, summer is coming up, and he is so excited for the Tilt-A-Whirl and fried dough and the flying swings.
<v Ira Glass>像往常一样感谢我们节目的联合创始人，托里·马拉蒂亚先生。你知道，夏天就要到了，他为倾斜旋转木马、炸面团和飞行秋千感到非常兴奋。

00:57:49.040 --> 00:57:51.050
<v Caller>I was a carnival baby.
<v Caller>我是个嘉年华宝宝。

00:57:51.050 --> 00:57:56.050
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周回到《美国生活》的更多故事。
